meta {
  name: Contract SQL — unknown configuration fields silently dropped [CONCERN]
  type: http
  seq: 51
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/datasources
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-Allowed-Scope-Ids: *
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "contract_sql_unknown_fields",
    "description": "Probes how unknown configuration keys are handled",
    "connectorType": "SQL",
    "configuration": {
      "driver": "postgres",
      "dsn": "postgres://example:5432/db",
      "user": "alice",
      "password": "s3cret",
      "table": "events",
      "columns": ["*"],
      "username": "alice-typo",
      "query": "SELECT 1"
    },
    "dataStructureVersionId": null
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.id) {
    bru.setVar('contractSqlUnknownFieldsId', res.body.id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("Status is 201 — unknown configuration keys do not cause a rejection", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });
  test("Recognised fields round-trip", function() {
    expect(res.body.configuration.driver).to.equal("postgres");
    expect(res.body.configuration.user).to.equal("alice");
    expect(res.body.configuration.table).to.equal("events");
  });
  test("Submitted `username` (typo of `user`) is silently dropped", function() {
    expect(res.body.configuration).to.not.have.property('username');
  });
  test("Submitted `query` (not part of the SQL connector schema) is silently dropped", function() {
    expect(res.body.configuration).to.not.have.property('query');
  });
}

docs {
  **CONTRACT CONCERN — distinct from the deferred-validation pattern.**

  Submitted configuration keys that do not match any property of the
  server-side `SqlConnectorConfiguration` value object are silently
  dropped. This test submits a valid SQL configuration alongside two
  unknown keys:

  - `username` — a plausible typo of the correct `user` field.
  - `query` — a name that is not part of the SQL connector schema at all.

  Both vanish from the response and never reach the database. The 201
  success status is not an artefact of deferred validation: these keys
  are unrecognised at deserialisation time and would still be dropped if
  the configuration were otherwise complete and `OnRelease`-valid.

  This is the default behaviour of Spring Boot's Jackson configuration
  (`spring.jackson.deserialization.fail-on-unknown-properties=false`).
  The user cannot tell that their input is being partially ignored —
  release-time validation cannot recover the dropped fields because by
  then they are already gone, and the OnRelease validator runs on the
  parsed value object, not on the raw request body.

  The connector-configuration refactor (#1391) should explicitly decide
  whether to:
  - Reject unknown configuration fields with a 400, or
  - Continue silently dropping them and document the canonical schema in
    the OpenAPI spec (`api/portal-backend/openapi.yaml`) so callers have a
    single source of truth.

  When the decision lands, this test will need to be inverted (expect 400)
  or updated (assert the dropped-but-warned behaviour).
}
